travel.stalled=Not getting closer to waypoint {index} in {seconds} s, {distance} blocks away: stopping and restoring.
travel.out-of-fireworks=You have run OUT of fireworks mid-flight: Baritone cannot keep boosting.
travel.low-fireworks=You have {count} fireworks left, the warning is set at {threshold}: restock or land.
travel.start-module-off=auto-travel is off: turn it on before launching a trip.
travel.start-already-travelling=A trip is already under way: stop it before launching another.
travel.start-no-world=No world loaded: nothing is launched.
travel.start-dead=You are dead: respawn before launching a trip, nobody flies from the death screen.
travel.start-no-baritone=Baritone is not loaded: this module flies with its commands and without it there is nothing to steer.
travel.start-no-fireworks=You are not carrying any fireworks: Baritone boosts with them and without any it does not take off. Nothing is launched. Those inside shulkers do not count: take them out first.
travel.start-no-elytra=You are not wearing an elytra: Baritone flies with it, and elytra-replace swaps the one you wear but does not put one on you. Put one on before launching.
travel.not-flying=Not flying: {reason}.
travel.launched=Trip launched with pattern {pattern}: {count} waypoints to {x}, {z}, {distance} blocks in a straight line.
travel.launched-no-position=Trip launched with pattern {pattern}: {count} waypoints, {distance} blocks in a straight line.
travel.sweep-running=nether-sweep has a sweep under way, and both steer the same Baritone: #elytra has a single goal, so this trip would take it away, the sweep would see its distance grow and at 45 s would stop with its own restoration -halting this trip midway and setting the rocket speed back to its resting value-, and this trip would diagnose a stall that does not exist. Besides, both borrow elytra-fly and elytra-replace on their own, so the second would record as your resting state what the first left behind. Finish the sweep or stop it with .xploits sweep stop, and then launch the trip.
travel.chest-swap-always=puts the chestplate in the elytra's slot that very instant, and the "{prefix}elytra" goes out two lines later: Baritone would not take off, and you would find out thirty seconds later from the stall cut-off
travel.chest-swap-wait-for-ground=leaves a listener armed that takes the elytra off you as soon as you touch the ground, which is exactly Baritone's landing: it would take it off when the module believes it has restored everything
travel.chest-swap-rejected=Not flying: elytra-fly is on with chest-swap set to {mode}, and the preparation has to turn it off because Baritone states its flight does not work with non-vanilla boosting. Turning it off {consequence}. Set chest-swap to Never inside elytra-fly, or turn elytra-fly off by hand before launching.
travel.undone=Not flying: preparing the environment left you without an elytra on, so Baritone could not take off. I undid the preparation and the environment is as it was.
travel.undone-not-restored=Not flying: preparing the environment left you without an elytra on, so Baritone could not take off. I undid the preparation, but {pending}.
travel.toast-undone-not-restored=The preparation was undone but did not reach Baritone: its settings were left at flight values. Read the chat.
travel.stop-not-travelling=No trip is under way.
travel.stop-restored=Trip stopped and environment restored.
travel.stop-not-restored=Trip stopped, but the environment was NOT restored: read the warning above.
travel.now-waypoint=waypoint {index}/{total}
travel.now-armed=armed
travel.finished=Trip over: {reason}. Environment restored.
travel.finished-not-restored=Trip over: {reason}, but the environment was NOT restored: {pending}.
travel.toast-finished-not-restored=The trip is over but the restoration did not reach Baritone: it may keep flying and its settings were left at flight values. Read the chat.
travel.modules-both={first} and {second}
travel.modules-left-as-in-flight=When leaving the world a Meteor module cannot be turned on or off without leaving it subscribed twice to the bus for the rest of the session, so {modules} stays as it was in flight. It is set back on its own on the first tick after joining again; if you close the client before that, check it in the ClickGUI.
travel.status-off=auto-travel is off.
travel.status-idle=auto-travel on, no trip under way. Pattern {pattern} · destination {destination}.
travel.status-flying=Flying with pattern {pattern} · waypoint {index} of {total}{position}{progress}\n  safety net: {net}{caught}
travel.status-at= at {x}, {z}
travel.status-progress= {distance} blocks away\n  {left} waypoints left after this one
travel.net-armed=armed
travel.net-disarmed=DISARMED
travel.status-net-caught= and it has already had to cancel a command: Baritone is not intercepting
travel.prefix-empty=Baritone's prefix is empty: the commands would go out to the server as plain chat -"set elytraAutoJump true" as is- and the net would have no way to recognise them. Set baritone-prefix to the prefix Baritone listens to, "#" by default
travel.prefix-blank=Baritone's prefix is only spaces: it does not tell a command apart from a sentence starting with a space, so neither would Baritone recognise it as its own nor could the net watch it. Set baritone-prefix to the prefix Baritone listens to, "#" by default
travel.prefix-slash=Baritone's prefix starts with a slash ("{prefix}"), and with a slash nothing works: the client sends everything starting with "/" down the command path, which is exactly the one Baritone does not hook, so the commands would go to the server instead of to Baritone; and the net would also cancel every other slash command while the trip lasted, auto-tpy's "/tpy" and kit-requester's whispers included. Set baritone-prefix to the prefix Baritone listens to, "#" by default
travel.restoration-cancelled=the restoration did NOT reach Baritone: the net had to cancel my own commands, so neither the "{prefix}cancel" nor the five settings left your client. Baritone may keep flying on its own, now with no stall watch, no firework warning and nobody to change its goal; and elytraAutoSwap, elytraAutoJump, elytraAllowEmergencyLand, elytraConserveFireworks and elytraFireworkSpeed were left at their flight values, which Baritone saves to disk for the next session. The net having to act means "{prefix}" is not the prefix Baritone listens to right now, or that no instance of it is bound to your player: type "cancel" by hand with the prefix Baritone really has -"#cancel" by default- and go over those five settings with "#set name value"
travel.restoration-no-player=the restoration did not go out: there was no longer a player to send anything to, so neither the "cancel" nor the five settings reached Baritone. elytraAutoSwap, elytraAutoJump, elytraAllowEmergencyLand, elytraConserveFireworks and elytraFireworkSpeed were left at their flight values, and Baritone saves them to disk for the next session: when you join again, go over them by hand with "{prefix}set name value" before relying on them
travel.name-period=the period
travel.name-leg=the leg
travel.name-amplitude=the amplitude
travel.name-offset=the lateral offset
travel.name-amplitude-effective=the effective amplitude
travel.name-offset-effective=the effective lateral offset
travel.name-radius=the radius
travel.why-spacing=it is twice waypoint-margin, which is at {margin} blocks, because Baritone starts landing {landing} blocks from its goal and it has to be changed before it gets there.
travel.why-spacing-floor=it is twice waypoint-margin, which is at {margin} blocks, because Baritone starts landing {landing} blocks from its goal and it has to be changed before it gets there, and never less than {floor}, which is the shortest a rocket-powered elytra flies as a leg rather than as a wobble.
travel.widen-corridor=The setting is {configured} blocks, but the highway corridor's maximum width caps it at 0: raise that width above 0 or choose STRAIGHT
travel.widen-setting=Raise {setting} above 0 blocks or choose STRAIGHT
travel.capped-by-corridor= -which also means raising the highway corridor's width, which currently caps it at {amplitude}-
travel.decoy-on-highway=the decoy would leave the highway corridor, so it is rejected rather than downgraded
travel.lateral-no-step={pattern} with {step} at {value} blocks leaves no progress between side changes: not a single wave would be drawn and the route would go straight to the destination without warning. Raise {step} above 0 blocks or choose STRAIGHT
travel.lateral-step-over-trip={pattern} with {step} at {value} blocks does not fit even once in a trip of {distance} blocks: not a single wave would be drawn and the route would go straight without warning. Lower {step} below {distance} blocks or choose STRAIGHT
travel.lateral-no-side={pattern} with {side} at {amplitude} blocks does not stray from the axis: the waypoints would all land on the straight line, a decorative pattern. {fix}
travel.lateral-too-many-sides={pattern} with {step} at {value} blocks would need {needed} side changes to weave a trip of {distance} blocks, and a route allows no more than {max}: the pattern would cover only the first {covered} blocks and the last {rest} would go in a straight line to the destination without warning, exactly the stretch that reaches home. Raise {step} to {minimum} blocks or more, which is where the whole pattern fits again, or choose STRAIGHT
travel.lateral-too-close={pattern} with {step} at {value} blocks and {side} at {amplitude} leaves its waypoints {gap} blocks apart, and {spacing} are needed: {why} With shorter gaps the module drops a waypoint and the next one in the same tick, the pattern is used up without being flown and the route goes straight without warning. Raise {step} to {neededStep} blocks, or {sideName} to {neededSide}{capped}, or choose STRAIGHT
travel.lateral-single-too-close={pattern} with {step} at {value} blocks fits only once in a trip of {distance}, and its only detour is {gap} blocks from the destination, less than the {spacing} needed: {why} Leaving it out would make the route straight and keeping it would make Baritone land twice. Raise {sideName} to {needed} blocks or more, move the destination further away, or choose STRAIGHT
travel.spiral-no-radius={pattern} with the effective radius at {radius} blocks makes no turn at all: all its steps would fall on the destination, so the approach would be straight. {fix}
travel.spiral-no-turns={pattern} with the turns at {turns} does not turn: its steps would all fall on the axis, between the destination and the point one radius before it, so the approach would be straight. Raise the turns above 0 or choose STRAIGHT
travel.spiral-too-close={pattern} with the effective radius at {radius} blocks and {turns} turns leaves steps as short as {step} blocks, and {spacing} are needed: {why} Removing the steps that cannot be flown leaves not one off the straight line to the destination, so the route would go straight without warning. {fix}
travel.spiral-fix-short-trip=A trip of {distance} blocks has no room for any flyable spiral -the radius cannot exceed half the trip-: move the destination further away, lower the turns, or choose another pattern
travel.spiral-fix-corridor=The radius is {radius} blocks, but the highway corridor's maximum width caps it at {max}: raise that width to {needed} blocks or more, or choose another pattern
travel.spiral-fix-radius=Raise the radius to {needed} blocks or more, or choose another pattern
travel.decoy-no-fraction={pattern} with the fraction at {fraction} covers nothing towards the decoy: the correction point would fall on the origin itself and the route would be the straight line to the destination. Raise the fraction above 0 or choose STRAIGHT
travel.decoy-no-angle={pattern} with the angle at {angle} degrees does not point off the axis: the correction point would fall on the origin-destination line itself and the route would be straight, fooling nobody. Give the angle a value that is not a multiple of 180 degrees or choose STRAIGHT
travel.decoy-too-close={pattern} with the angle at {angle} degrees and the fraction at {fraction} leaves a leg of {gap} blocks in a trip of {distance}, and {spacing} are needed: {why} With a shorter leg Baritone would land on the correction point instead of passing through it. Move the destination to {needed} blocks or more, raise the fraction, or choose STRAIGHT

# sweep
sweep.module-desc=Sweeps a Nether rectangle with lawnmower lanes so the terrain passes in front of the client, flying only what NewerNewChunks has not recorded yet. It detects nothing: BaseFinder, stash-finder and NewerNewChunks take care of that. Turning it on does not fly: the sweep is launched with .xploits sweep go.
sweep.setting-chunk-x-1=One corner of the rectangle, X coordinate in Nether CHUNKS (the block one divided by 16).
sweep.setting-chunk-z-1=One corner of the rectangle, Z coordinate in Nether CHUNKS.
sweep.setting-chunk-x-2=The opposite corner, X coordinate in Nether CHUNKS. The order does not matter: it is normalised.
sweep.setting-chunk-z-2=The opposite corner, Z coordinate in Nether CHUNKS.
sweep.setting-lane-width=Spacing between lanes, in chunks. Leave it at 0 and it is MEASURED from the chunk stream the server sends, which is the right thing (spec §5). Any other value fixes it by hand and turns the measurement off: only if you know the server's real reach, because overshooting leaves strips unseen and the sweep marks them as combed anyway.
sweep.setting-lane-width-margin=What fraction of the measured radius is taken off as a margin. Flying fast leaves gaps even when the nominal distance is right, because chunks take time to arrive: 0.2 keeps 80 % of the observed radius. Not used if lane-width is set by hand.
sweep.setting-waypoint-margin=How many blocks before each vertex Baritone's goal is changed, so it has no time to land on it. A sweep has two vertices per lane, so without this it would be dozens of landings.
sweep.setting-firework-reserve=Margin over the fireworks the projection says are needed. 0.2 cuts when there are 20 % fewer than needed, that is with fireworks still in hand and not when you hit zero.
sweep.setting-count-return-trip=Whether the firework budget includes the trip back from the end of the sweep to where you took off. Turning it off does not give you more fireworks: it only stops counting them, and you find out they are not enough when you are already far away.
sweep.setting-blocks-per-firework=The consumption measured in earlier sweeps, which is where the estimate from BEFORE take-off comes from (spec §6). The module writes it at the end of each sweep; 0 means there is no measurement yet, and then it says so instead of showing a made-up number.
sweep.setting-no-projection-grace=How many blocks it keeps flying without being able to project the fireworks before cutting. It happens at the start -until the first measurement- and if you restock more often than it measures, which makes the data expire. Flying on without a projection is flying without the firework protection, so it cuts instead of keeping quiet. The minimum is three sampling intervals because a rate needs two samples: below that, the cut comes before the first possible measurement.
sweep.setting-baritone-prefix=The prefix Baritone reads its commands with. Only change it if you have changed it in Baritone.
sweep.setting-auto-jump=elytraAutoJump during the sweep: Baritone takes off on its own.
sweep.setting-auto-jump-resting=The value elytraAutoJump is set back to at the end. It has to be declared: Baritone's settings can be written but not read. Baritone ships it as false.
sweep.setting-emergency-land=elytraAllowEmergencyLand during the sweep: an emergency landing rather than a crash.
sweep.setting-emergency-land-resting=The value elytraAllowEmergencyLand is set back to at the end. Baritone ships it as true.
sweep.setting-conserve-fireworks=elytraConserveFireworks during the sweep: use fewer fireworks in exchange for going slower. In a sweep of hours it usually pays off.
sweep.setting-conserve-fireworks-resting=The value elytraConserveFireworks is set back to at the end. Baritone ships it as false: set it to true only if you had it that way, because Baritone saves it to disk and all your manual flights would be slower from the first sweep on.
sweep.setting-firework-speed=elytraFireworkSpeed during the sweep. Flying faster than when the lane width was measured opens gaps: that is what lane-width-margin is for.
sweep.setting-firework-speed-resting=The value elytraFireworkSpeed is set back to at the end. Baritone ships it as 1.2.
sweep.setting-nether-seed=The Nether seed, if known. Empty is left alone: without a seed Baritone turns terrain prediction off by itself, which is the right thing.
sweep.setting-notify=Local notice on launch, on changing lanes and on finishing. The loud warnings -the safety net, the stall, the fireworks and the detectors being off- always show, whether you turn this off or not.
sweep.setting-coverage-floor=What fraction of the area has to have really arrived for the sweep to count as good when it ends. Below this the final notice is LOUD instead of an info: a sweep that covered half cannot look like one that covered everything, because both end and only one has to be repeated. Relaunching the same rectangle replans over the gaps left.
sweep.setting-notify-sound=Sound on the loud warnings.
sweep.nothing=
sweep.armed=Armed, but it does not fly on its own: launch the sweep with .xploits sweep go
sweep.measuring=Meanwhile I am measuring how far the server sends chunks, which is where the lane width comes from.
sweep.render-distance-changed=The effective render distance went from {from} to {to} chunks: I am throwing away the lane width measurement and taking it again.
sweep.net-caught=Baritone is NOT intercepting its commands: I cancelled "{command}" before it went out to the server. Anything you type by hand with that prefix WOULD be published.
sweep.net-caught-verb=Baritone is NOT intercepting its commands: I cancelled a «{verb}» before it went out to the server. Anything you type by hand with that prefix WOULD be published.
sweep.reason-module-off=nether-sweep was turned off
sweep.reason-left-world=you left the world
sweep.reason-lost-world=the world was lost
sweep.reason-died=you died mid-sweep
sweep.reason-done=the sweep is complete
sweep.reason-stall=stalled
sweep.reason-no-projection=I cannot project the fireworks
sweep.reason-out-of-fireworks=the fireworks will not last
sweep.reason-cancelled=you cancelled it
sweep.lane-progress=Lane {lane} of {total}.
sweep.stalled=Not getting closer to vertex {index} in {seconds} s, {distance} blocks away: stopping and restoring.
sweep.out-of-fireworks=You have run OUT of fireworks mid-sweep: Baritone cannot keep boosting.
sweep.low-fireworks=You have {count} fireworks left, the warning is set at {threshold}: restock or stop the sweep.
sweep.no-projection-warning=I have flown {flown} blocks without being able to measure firework consumption: either none has been used yet, or you restock so often that the measurement has expired. Flying WITHOUT a firework projection; if this goes on for {left} more blocks, I stop.
sweep.no-projection-cut=I have flown {flown} blocks without being able to project the fireworks and no-projection-grace is at {grace}: stopping the sweep. Going on would be flying without the only protection you have against being stranded far from home, and keeping quiet about it would be worse than stopping.
sweep.fireworks-short=The fireworks will NOT last: at the measured rate of {rate} blocks per firework you have {remaining} blocks ahead{return}, which with firework-reserve at {reserve} % is about {needed} fireworks, and you carry {count}. I am stopping the sweep here instead of leaving you stranded further away. The two settings behind this count are firework-reserve, the margin over what is needed, and count-return-trip, which right now {counts} the trip back to where you took off -turning it off does not give you more fireworks, it only stops counting them-.
sweep.fireworks-short-with-return= counting the trip back
sweep.fireworks-short-without-return= WITHOUT counting the trip back
sweep.return-counted=DOES count
sweep.return-not-counted=does NOT count
sweep.start-module-off=nether-sweep is off: turn it on before launching a sweep.
sweep.start-already-sweeping=A sweep is already under way: stop it before launching another.
sweep.start-no-world=No world loaded: nothing is launched.
sweep.start-dead=You are dead: respawn before launching a sweep, nobody flies from the death screen.
sweep.start-not-nether=You are not in the Nether: this module sweeps the Nether because there every block flown covers 64 times more Overworld surface, and everything it announces -the equivalent area, the reason for flying this- rests on that count. Go through a portal and launch it again.
sweep.start-no-baritone=Baritone is not loaded: this module flies with its commands and without it there is nothing to steer.
sweep.start-no-fireworks=You are not carrying any fireworks: Baritone boosts with them and without any it does not take off. Nothing is launched. Those inside shulkers do not count: take them out first.
sweep.start-no-elytra=You are not wearing an elytra: Baritone flies with it, and elytra-replace swaps the one you wear but does not put one on you. Put one on before launching.
sweep.not-sweeping=Not sweeping: {reason}
sweep.not-sweeping-prefix=Not sweeping: {reason}.
sweep.nothing-to-sweep=Nothing to sweep: the area's {chunks} chunks are already fully seen according to NewerNewChunks ({reading}). {equivalent}
sweep.unknown-server=I could not tell which server you are on, so I did NOT read NewerNewChunks' coverage: this is NOT that nothing is recorded, it is that I did not even look. I am going to plan the whole rectangle, so if you had already seen part of it you will repeat it.
sweep.typed-width=lane-width is typed by hand at {width} chunks, so the sweep does NOT measure the lane width: if the server sends less than that, strips will be left unseen and this sweep will count them as combed anyway. Set lane-width to 0 so it measures itself.
sweep.launched=Sweep launched: {lanes} lanes {width} chunks wide ({how}) over the area's {chunks} chunks, of which {seen} were already seen ({reading}).\n  Flight: {approach} blocks of approach + {sweep} of sweep{return} = {total} blocks.\n  Equivalent coverage: {equivalent}.\n  {estimate}
sweep.launched-return= + {blocks} back
sweep.width-typed=typed
sweep.width-measured=measured
sweep.not-enough-samples=Not sweeping yet: the lane width is measured from the chunk stream the server sends, and I only have {samples} of the {needed} samples needed{discarded}. Leave the module on and take a WALK around so the server sends you terrain -flying does not work: at that speed each chunk arrives when you are already far from where the server queued it, and that measures its queue and not its reach-. If you know its real reach, set it by hand in lane-width, bearing in mind that nothing is measured there any more.
sweep.lane-too-short=Not sweeping: the shortest lane of the route is {lane} blocks long and with waypoint-margin at {margin} more than {minimum} are needed. A lane like that is consumed as soon as the vertex that starts it is released, so it would NEVER be flown and the sweep would count it as combed anyway. Lanes run end to end, so the shortest one measures the long side of the area: enlarge the rectangle along it -move chunk-x-1 and chunk-x-2 apart, or chunk-z-1 and chunk-z-2, whichever pair is closer together- until it is over {minimum} blocks, which is {chunks} chunks. Lowering waypoint-margin also works{fix}.
sweep.lane-too-short-lower-margin= below {lane}, its minimum being {min}
sweep.lane-too-short-margin-not-enough=, but that is not enough: its minimum is {min} and the lane is already below it
sweep.link-inside-margin=The shortest link between two lanes is {link} blocks long and waypoint-margin is at {margin}, so that vertex will be consumed without being flown: Baritone will not take the corner, it will go diagonally from the end of one lane to the end of the next. The strip is still crossed -no lane is lost-, but its edges will pass further from the client than planned. If you want clean corners, lower waypoint-margin or lower lane-width-margin, which widens the lanes and spreads the strips apart.
sweep.link-too-short=The shortest link between two lanes is {link} blocks long, under the {spacing} an elytra flies as a leg: there Baritone will overshoot and come back for the vertex, so the sweep will be slower and use more fireworks. No lane is lost, so I fly anyway.
sweep.auto-travel-running=auto-travel has a trip under way, and both steer the same Baritone: #elytra has a single goal, so the sweep would take it away, auto-travel would see its distance grow and at 45 s would stop with its own restoration -halting the sweep midway and setting the rocket speed back to its resting value-, and the sweep would diagnose a stall that does not exist. Besides, both borrow elytra-fly and elytra-replace on their own, so the second would record as your resting state what the first left behind. Finish the trip or stop it with .xploits travel stop, and then launch the sweep.
sweep.estimate-no-data=Estimated fireworks: no data. I have never measured your consumption per block, and showing you a made-up number that looks like a measurement would be worse than telling you. You carry {count} fireworks; the real projection arrives within a minute of flight, once the measurement has something to go on.
sweep.estimate-enough=Estimated fireworks: about {needed} with the reserve, at {rate} blocks per firework measured in earlier sweeps. You carry {count}, which is enough.
sweep.estimate-short=Estimated fireworks: about {needed} with the reserve, at {rate} blocks per firework measured in earlier sweeps, and you carry {count}: you are SHORT by about {missing}. I take off anyway because the estimate is old and this flight's measurement overrides it, but I will stop as soon as the real projection says they will not last.
sweep.detector-newer-new-chunks=without it I neither use the coverage you already have -I replan the whole area and you repeat terrain already seen- nor is any trace of this sweep left for next time
sweep.detector-base-finder=without it this sweep covers terrain and records no base: hours of flight for nothing
sweep.detector-stash-finder=without it this sweep covers terrain and records no container: hours of flight for nothing
sweep.detector-not-installed=is not installed
sweep.detector-off=is off
sweep.detector-warning={name} {state}, and {consequence}. This module flies; it detects nothing.
sweep.chest-swap-always=puts your chestplate in the elytra's place at that very moment, and the "{prefix}elytra" goes out two lines later: Baritone would not take off, and you would find out from the stall cut
sweep.chest-swap-wait-for-ground=leaves a listener armed that takes your elytra off as soon as you touch the ground, which is exactly Baritone's landing: it would take it off when the module believes it has restored everything
sweep.chest-swap-rejected=Not sweeping: elytra-fly is on with chest-swap at {mode}, and the preparation has to turn it off because Baritone states its flight does not work with non-vanilla boosting. Turning it off {consequence}. Set chest-swap to Never inside elytra-fly, or turn elytra-fly off by hand before launching.
sweep.undone=Not sweeping: preparing the environment left you without an elytra on, so Baritone could not take off. I undid the preparation and the environment is as it was.
sweep.undone-not-restored=Not sweeping: preparing the environment left you without an elytra on, so Baritone could not take off. I undid the preparation, but {pending}.
sweep.toast-undone-not-restored=The preparation was undone but did not reach Baritone: its settings were left at flight values. Read the chat.
sweep.stop-not-sweeping=There is no sweep under way.
sweep.stop-restored=Sweep stopped and environment restored.
sweep.stop-not-restored=Sweep stopped, but the environment was NOT restored: read the warning above.
sweep.now-lane=lane {lane}/{total}
sweep.now-armed=armed
sweep.finished=Sweep over: {reason}. Environment restored.{coverage}{relaunch}
sweep.finished-not-restored=Sweep over: {reason}, but the environment was NOT restored: {pending}.{coverage}{relaunch}
sweep.finished-coverage= {summary}.
sweep.finished-relaunch= Relaunch the same rectangle when you can: NewerNewChunks has been recording what did arrive, so the sweep is replanned only over the gaps left.
sweep.toast-finished-not-restored=The sweep is over but the restoration did not reach Baritone: it may keep flying and its settings were left at flight values. Read the chat.
sweep.toast-short-of-coverage=The sweep is over without {missing} of the area's chunks arriving: it is NOT fully combed. Relaunch it over the same rectangle and only what is missing will be flown.
sweep.modules-both={first} and {second}
sweep.modules-left-as-in-flight=When leaving the world a Meteor module cannot be turned on or off without leaving it subscribed twice to the bus for the rest of the session, so {modules} stays as it was in flight. It is given back on its own on the first tick after joining again; if you close the client before that, check it in the ClickGUI.
sweep.status-off=nether-sweep is off.
sweep.status-idle=nether-sweep on, no sweep under way.\n  lane width: {width}\n  consumption measured in earlier sweeps: {rate}
sweep.status-rate={rate} blocks per firework
sweep.status-no-rate=no data yet
sweep.status-sweeping=Sweeping: lane {lane} of {total} · width {width} chunks ({how}){coverage}\n  flown {flown} blocks{left}\n  this flight's consumption: {rate}\n  safety net: {net}{caught}
sweep.status-coverage=\n  {summary}
sweep.status-left=, {blocks} left{return}
sweep.status-with-return= counting the trip back
sweep.status-without-return= not counting the trip back
sweep.status-no-flight-rate=no measurement right now, {blocks} blocks without being able to project
sweep.net-armed=armed
sweep.net-disarmed=DISARMED
sweep.status-net-caught= and it has already had to cancel a command: Baritone is not intercepting
sweep.width-typed-desc={width} chunks, TYPED by hand (nothing is measured)
sweep.width-measuring=still being measured, {samples} of {needed} samples of the chunk stream so far{discarded}
sweep.width-measured-desc={width} chunks, measured (observed radius {radius} chunks out of a ceiling of {ceiling}, margin {margin,2}){discarded}
sweep.discarded-late=; I discarded {count} chunks that arrived late -beyond the render distance, so they did not measure the server's reach-
sweep.discarded-moving=; and {count} that arrived while you were moving -they measured how far you had moved while the packet was queued, not how far the server sends-
sweep.discarded={late}{moving}
sweep.reading-unknown-server=I did NOT read the coverage: I could not tell which server you are on
sweep.reading-none=no NewerNewChunks file for this server and dimension: starting from scratch
sweep.reading-files={read} of {total} NewerNewChunks files
sweep.reading-files-broken={read} of {total} NewerNewChunks files, {broken} unreadable
sweep.unreadable-file=I could not read NewerNewChunks' {file} ({error}): carrying on with the rest, but the terrain that file recorded will be replanned as if it had not been seen.
sweep.oversize=The area is {width}x{height} chunks, {total} in total, and the cap is {max}. Nothing is trimmed behind your back: a sweep that kept a piece of the rectangle to itself and then said «finished» would leave unseen terrain counted as combed, which is exactly what this module exists not to do. {fix}. Bring chunk-x-1 closer to chunk-x-2, or chunk-z-1 closer to chunk-z-2. To get an idea: the cap is more than twenty hours of flight, and the whole box you have crossed in months fits twenty times inside it, so going over is usually one digit too many when typing a coordinate.
sweep.oversize-nothing-fits=Even keeping the short side, of {short} chunks, nothing fits: bring the two corners closer on both axes
sweep.oversize-keep-short=Keeping the short side at {short} chunks, the other cannot go over {max}
sweep.overworld-equivalent={width}x{height} Overworld blocks
sweep.unusable-lane-width=The sweep with the lane width -the lane-width setting- at {width} chunks {effect}. Quietly downgrading it to 1 chunk would be worse than stopping: the sweep would fly with a made-up spacing, leave strips unseen and mark them as combed anyway. Raise lane-width to 1 chunk or more. It is not normally typed -it is left at 0 and measured from the chunk stream the server sends-, so if it got here at {width} the measurement has no samples yet or lane-width-margin left it there.
sweep.lane-width-zero=does not advance a single strip: each lane would land on top of the previous one and the area's route would never end
sweep.lane-width-negative=goes backwards: the strips would leave the area through the opposite edge and the rectangle would never be fully covered
sweep.coverage-complete=Area coverage: {covered} of {total} chunks ({percent} %), {seen} already seen when planning and {arrived} that arrived during the sweep. None missing
sweep.coverage-missing=Area coverage: {covered} of {total} chunks ({percent} %), {seen} already seen when planning and {arrived} that arrived during the sweep. {missing} chunks of the area NEVER arrived

# pvp
pvp.module-desc=Steers Meteor's combat modules according to the phase of the fight.
pvp.setting-target-range=How many blocks away a target is searched for. Sorting into a phase is something else and is fixed at 10: no steered module reaches further, so a target beyond 10 is seen and reported, but turns nothing on.
pvp.setting-approach-distance=Border between approach and surface, with a one-block band on each side: it goes into APPROACH above this distance plus 1 and back to SURFACE below this minus 1, so a target standing right on the threshold does not make the phase flicker. Capped at 6: Meteor's EntityUtils.getCityBlock() does not see surrounded beyond that distance, and a larger approach-distance would leave a strip where SURROUNDED is never detected. Floor at 2: below 3 no phase asks for the aura any more, and from 3 to crystal range (4.5) it is kept on by the hostile count, not by the phase.
pvp.setting-threat-margin=How much health you must have left, after subtracting the damage ALREADY aimed at you (placed crystals, someone with a sword right next to you, beds in the Nether, falling), to stay calm. Below it hole-filler, anti-anvil, anti-bed and anti-anchor turn on, plus surround if you are in a hole, standing on the ground and with your height steady -while your Y moves, surround would turn itself off-. It is not 'my health is low': it is what is already placed against you. Raising it triggers sooner and costs little -none of those modules roots you and only hole-filler spends anything-; lowering it lets you react later.
pvp.setting-sync-friends=WRITES TO YOUR METEOR FRIENDS LIST (.friends), which does not belong to the addon: while auto-pvp is on it adds kit-requester's couriers and auto-tpy's users list there, and removes them when it is turned off. It is the only way for crystal-aura, auto-trap, auto-web, auto-anvil and auto-city -which pick their own target and only look at that list- not to attack them either. It only removes what it added: a friend you already had is never touched, and if you remove one of its own by hand it does not put it back. With trust-unknown-couriers on it syncs NO courier, because then anyone who imitates a READY gets into that list by themselves (spec §14.2).
pvp.setting-notify=Local notice on a change of offensive phase or defensive posture, when something the situation asked for is not turned on, when one of ours is not attacked and when your Meteor friends list is touched. Each notice is said once, when it appears, not every tick. The two silent-failure notices -OUT OF RESOURCES and 'on for a while without spending anything'- are always said, whether this is on or not: they are exactly the ones that cannot be seen any other way.
pvp.setting-notify-sound=Sound on the loud OUT OF RESOURCES notice.
